In the legacy configuration, {legacy_bfs_unreach_20k} nodes were unreachable by BFS from the entry point at N=20,000 (data seed {decomp_seed}), while on average {legacy_unreach_mean} nodes per index failed 1-NN self-retrieval across the 25-seed matrix. Most failing nodes were therefore connected to the graph but were not found by greedy search. The cured configuration has {cured_bfs_unreach_20k} BFS-unreachable nodes at the same size. BFS reachability does not predict self-retrieval failures; this supports the navigability hypothesis over pure connectivity loss. The two metrics come from different runs and seeds, so this is supporting evidence, not a direct test.
